Drop -Werror from configure.ac's CFLAGS, for the gcc this chain builds with.

WHY: swtpm's configure.ac appends -Werror to CFLAGS unconditionally
(configure.ac:545), exactly as libtpms does in AM_CFLAGS -- same author,
same flag set -- and libtpms died on it in run 88777336449 the moment
the stage-4 gcc's gnu23 default met a const-correct strstr(). swtpm is
package 142 in the plan; waiting to measure the identical failure forty
packages later costs a runner-hour to learn what the sibling package
already taught. Read from the pinned tarball (sha256 f8da11ca...), not
recalled: the line is the second of four that build swtpm's warning set.

The same ruling this chain made for the kernel (CONFIG_WERROR off) and
that Gentoo carries for this package as
swtpm-0.10.0-build-sys-Remove-WError.patch. The warnings stay visible in
the log; they stop being fatal.

WHERE: configure.ac, not the generated configure -- the recipe runs
autoreconf after unpack and the driver applies patches before that. One
hunk, generated by diff -u against the pinned tarball, not written by
hand; proven to apply with the same `patch -p1 -N` the driver uses.

--- a/configure.ac
+++ b/configure.ac
@@ -542,7 +542,7 @@
 AC_SUBST([TSS_GROUP])
 
 CFLAGS="$CFLAGS -Wreturn-type -Wsign-compare -Wswitch-enum"
-CFLAGS="$CFLAGS -Wmissing-prototypes -Wall -Werror"
+CFLAGS="$CFLAGS -Wmissing-prototypes -Wall"
 CFLAGS="$CFLAGS -Wformat -Wformat-security -Wshadow"
 CFLAGS="$CFLAGS $GNUTLS_CFLAGS $COVERAGE_CFLAGS $SANITIZER_CFLAGS"
 
